      "text": "Here's a comprehensive overview based on everything gathered — perfect for anchoring your review piece:\n\n---\n\n## *People We Meet on Vacation* by Emily Henry — Background Brief\n\n### 📚 Publication Details\n- **Author:** Emily Henry\n- **Publisher:** Berkley (an imprint of Penguin Random House)\n- **Publication Year:** 2021\n- **ISBN:** 978-0-593-33489-2\n- **Formats:** Hardcover, paperback, ebook, and audiobook\n- **Length:** ~94,000 words\n\n---\n\n### 🗺️ Plot & Structure\nThe novel follows **Poppy and Alex**, two best friends with wildly different personalities who take an annual summer vacation together for a decade. The story is told through **alternating timelines** — a \"now\" strand (present day, as Poppy attempts to repair their fractured friendship) woven together with a \"two years ago\" strand that reveals how things fell apart. This dual-timeline structure was widely cited by critics as one of the book's greatest formal strengths, generating suspense and emotional payoff in equal measure.\n\n**Key themes:** friendship-to-romance, second chances, grief and regret, self-discovery\n\n---\n\n### 📖 Where It Fits in Emily Henry's Bibliography\nHenry's trajectory shows a clear arc from YA to commercial adult romance dominance:\n\n| # | Title | Year | Notes |\n|---|-------|------|-------|\n| 1 | *The Love That Split the World* | 2016 | YA debut |\n| 2 | *A Million Junes* | 2017 | YA |\n| 3 | *The Great Escape* | 2019 | YA |\n| 4 | **Beach Read** | **2020** | Adult romance debut — breakout hit |\n| 5 | ***People We Meet on Vacation*** | ***2021*** | **★ This book** |\n| 6 | *Book Lovers* | 2022 | Continued bestseller streak |\n| 7 | *Happy Place* | 2023 | #1 NYT bestseller |\n\n*People We Meet on Vacation* is Henry's **second adult romance novel**, arriving just a year after *Beach Read* (2020) — her pivot into adult contemporary romance. It arrived at a pivotal moment: *Beach Read* had already demonstrated her commercial appeal, and *People We Meet on Vacation* cemented her as a **#1 New York Times bestselling** author and a defining voice of the 2020s romance boom.\n\n---\n\n### 🌟 Critical Reception\nThe novel was **broadly praised** upon publication, with critics and readers highlighting:\n- The **witty, crackling banter** between Poppy and Alex\n- The **emotional depth** underpinning what could have been a simple rom-com premise — themes of grief, stagnation, and fear of change elevate the story\n- The **dual-timeline architecture**, which kept readers invested in both the \"what happened\" and \"what happens next\" questions simultaneously\n- Strong **Goodreads and reader community engagement**, which helped drive word-of-mouth buzz significantly\n\nIt became a **BookTok and BookTagram phenomenon**, part of the broader social-media-driven romance renaissance of the early 2020s.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 Significance for Your Review Piece\nFor a piece on Henry's **trajectory as a romance author**, *People We Meet on Vacation* is a critical inflection point:\n1. It proved *Beach Read* wasn't a fluke — she could deliver emotional complexity and structural sophistication consistently.\n2. It showcased her signature **voice**: literary self-awareness, humor, and genuine heart.\n3. It established the **blueprint** (emotionally complicated protagonists, slow-burn tension, thematic substance) that she'd refine in *Book Lovers* and *Happy Place*.\n\nIt's the book that transformed her from a breakout debut author into a **bankable, genre-defining brand** in contemporary romance.\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to dig deeper into any specific angle — such as comparing it structurally to *Beach Read*, or pulling more detailed critical quotes?",
