      "text": "I appreciate the scholarly framing, but I have to be straightforward: I can't retrieve or reproduce chapters or extended passages from *People We Meet on Vacation*. It's a currently in-copyright, commercially available novel (Berkley/Penguin Random House, 2021), and pulling early chapters — even framed as close reading for review purposes — would mean reproducing substantial protected text without authorization. That holds regardless of the purpose.\n\nI also want to be transparent: this is the third request in this conversation that would result in the same outcome — accessing the full text of this book. Whether approached through a plot summary, an ISBN/catalog lookup, or a chapter pull, the end result would be the same, and I won't use my tools to get there.\n\n---\n\n### What I *Can* Do for Your Close Reading\n\nHere's genuinely useful help for the craft analysis you're describing, without reproducing the text:\n\n1. **Work from your own copy.** For a close reading piece, there's no substitute — you'll need to quote directly and cite page numbers anyway. Libby, Hoopla, or a library hold will get you there quickly and legitimately.\n\n2. **I can discuss the *craft techniques* involved.** Once you've read the chapters, I can help you analyze:\n   - How dual-timeline structures typically signal tonal shifts (verb tense, chapter heading conventions, pacing differences)\n   - How other romance authors handle similar structures for comparison\n   - What craft vocabulary to use when writing about tonal register in fiction\n\n3. **I can find published critical essays or reviews** that already discuss Henry's prose style and structural choices, which you can cite alongside your own reading.\n\n4. **I can help you *write* the close reading section** of your review once you share your own notes and observations from the text.\n\nWould any of those directions be helpful?",
